"I'm scared there's no safe place for my bitcoin."

Security is not a binary attribute. You are always making trade-offs between convenience of your own access &amp; inconvenience of unauthorized access.

The only perfectly secure BTC is that which you send to an unspendable script.

@TeddyE_ If you're gonna do 20 you might as well do 100.

@ibenaija That won't help anything. Everyone makes mistakes; bitcoin users should assume that vendors will screw up and architect their setup to be robust against such failures.
https://t.co/ODsIcknrQD

Should you lose faith in self custody due to recent events? I think not.

Third party custodians are also susceptible to weak RNGs. Interestingly enough, COLDCARD is one of the few devices that support dice rolls (along with seedsigner and krux.)
https://t.co/tjUCIt2zoR

@pozzeke @BitBoxSwiss Ah this seems to be slightly different (dice input doesn't happen on device) which is why my LLM didn't list it.

@m1k__3 Security is not a binary attribute. Nothing is perfectly safe from absolutely every threat. The goal is to make yourself safe from as much as is reasonably possible.

@muneeb Absolutely; by using different hardware vendors you tend to get that automatically as long as they aren't forks of each other.

@snieders @CasaHODL Once you have a new vendor device initialized with a freshly generated seed phrase then all you need to do in the Casa app is select the key you want to replace and mark it as compromised; it will walk you through the process of doing a key rotation and funds transfer.

@delvin_baronof @CasaHODL Yes, though you should plan on rotating out those keys at your convenience, it's just not a drop-all-things-right-now emergency.

@WalshSRCM @CasaHODL Correct

@0xa7e8 @CasaHODL I'm not a fan of passphrases because I've seen many folks lock themselves out of keys by forgetting them. If you're going to go that route, make sure you have plenty of backups of the passphrase.

